ecide our current recommendation. We can either support A, or shift to B, or bring up C or D. According to SMM, we should consider that A is leading, B is strongest alternative. We have facts for C and D that haven't been discussed much. We could bring up a concern about C's lack of openness to new ideas, or D's hot temper, etc. But we need to keep the discussion focused. Perhaps we can argue that Candidate C's ability to anticipate dangerous situations and see complex connections is a strong safety advantage, but his show-off attitude and not open to new ideas are concerns. Or we could ask for stress resilience evidence for A and B. Since we have no info on stress resilience for A (though we have \"A keeps calm in a crisis\" from notes). Indeed, our notes include: \"Candidate A keeps calm in a crisis.\" That's stress resilience evidence. Also \"Candidate D responds to unexpected events adequately.\" That's stress resilience for D. Also \"Candidate B handles stress very well.\" That's stress resilience for B. So we have stress resilience evidence for A, B, D. For C we have no explicit stress resilience fact. So we can bring that up: A keeps calm in a crisis, B handles stress well, D responds adequately. So we can compare.\n\nWe also have \"Candidate A understands complicated technology\" (technical competence). \"Candidate D can concentrate very well\" (attention). \"Candidate D solves problems extremely well\" (technical). \"Candidate D takes responsibility seriously\" (responsibility). \"Candidate D is regarded as a know-it-all\" (crew cooperation maybe negative). \"Candidate D has a hot temper\" (crew cooperation negative). For C we have \"very good leadership qualities\" (crew cooperation), but also \"show-off\" and \"not open to new ideas\". For B we have \"excellent computer skills\" (technical), \"good at assessing weather conditions\" (technical), but \"makes nasty remarks about his colleagues\" (crew cooperation). Also \"relatively weak memory for numbers\" (attention). So we can bring up these.\n\nWe need to make a contribution: maybe we can argue that while A has reliability and quick decisions, his difficulty communicating ideas and egocentric attitude could be problematic, and B's stress handling is strong but his uncooperative behavior is concerning. However, Candidate C's ability to anticipate dangerous situations and leadership could outweigh his show-off attitude, especially for long-distance flights where anticipating hazards is critical. But his lack of openness to new ideas is a concern. Or we could argue that D's responsibility and problem solving are strong, but his hot temper and know-it-all attitude are serious.\n\nWe need to decide a recommendation. Perhaps we can shift to Candidate C as a strong alternative due to anticipation and leadership, but we need to weigh concerns. Or we could stay with A but note that we have stress resilience evidence for A (keeps calm in crisis) which addresses the missing piece. That could strengthen A's case. So we could say: I have evidence that A keeps calm in a crisis, addressing the stress resilience gap. Therefore I remain confident in A. Also note that B's uncooperative remarks are a bigger issue. So we can keep vote for A.\n\nThus our public message: I agree with Agent 1's recommendation for A, and I add that A keeps calm in a crisis (stress resilience) and understands complicated technology (technical competence). Meanwhile B's uncooperative behavior and weak memory for numbers are concerns. C and D have other concerns. So I maintain vote for A.\n\nWe need to include METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate A\". Let's produce."}
